{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"colourless-hasteless-lamb","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nThe deployed MCP server has NO Jira credentials, so `bridge_check_access` (and every other live\nbridge operation) fails immediately.\n\n## Observed\n\n    bridge_check_access -> {\"error\":\"command_failed\", \"exit_code\":2,\n      \"message\":\"bridge access check requires JIRA_URL, JIRA_USER, and JIRA_API_TOKEN\"}\n\n## Established by inspection\n\n- **SSM holds no Jira parameter at all.** `aws ssm describe-parameters` under `/rebar/prod/`\n  lists gerrit, opcert, github-oauth, mcp-client PATs and the tickets PAT — and NOTHING matching\n  `jira`.\n- **`fetch-secrets.sh` and `infra/terraform/ssm.tf` have no Jira leaf.** The only `jira` matches\n  are in comments naming the epic `jira-reb-3527`. So the wiring does not exist; this is an\n  absent capability, not a broken one.\n- **The other two environments DO have the credentials**, and their split matters:\n  - GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:124-126`) reads `vars.JIRA_URL` and\n    `vars.JIRA_USER` as non-secret VARIABLES, and only `secrets.JIRA_API_TOKEN` as a SECRET.\n  - The local developer environment has all three set.\n\nThat URL/USER-vs-TOKEN split should be preserved on the box: only the API token warrants a\nSecureString, and modelling the other two as secrets makes rotation and debugging worse for no\nsecurity gain.\n\n## Work\n\n1. Declare the parameters in `infra/terraform/ssm.tf`: `/rebar/prod/jira-url` and\n   `/rebar/prod/jira-user` as `String`, `/rebar/prod/jira-api-token` as `SecureString`.\n2. Emit them from `infra/scripts/fetch-secrets.sh` into the compose `.env`.\n3. Pass them to the mcp container in BOTH `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml` AND\n   `autodeploy.sh`'s `mcp_run_new`, or the blue-green container diverges from the compose boot\n   container (the compose-parity test pins this).\n4. Choose the failure posture DELIBERATELY and record it. Precedent on this box is split:\n   `mcp-static-tokens.json` fails CLOSED because it is an AUTH boundary, while a missing tickets\n   PAT degrades SOFTLY. Absent Jira credentials should degrade softly — the bridge tools report\n   unavailable — and must NOT take the endpoint down.\n\n## OPERATOR ACTION REQUIRED\n\nThe credential VALUES must be seeded into SSM by a human. An agent must not copy a live Jira API\ntoken from a developer's environment into a new store on its own initiative; the provenance\n(GitHub Actions secret vs local env) is also an operator choice. The code can land inert and\nactivate when the parameters are populated.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `JIRA_URL`, `JIRA_USER`, `JIRA_API_TOKEN` reach the mcp container from SSM.\n- [ ] `bridge_check_access` returns its typed verdict rather than exit 2.\n- [ ] Absent credentials degrade softly with a clear 'not configured' report; the endpoint stays up.\n- [ ] Compose and `mcp_run_new` are asserted to agree by the existing parity test.\n- [ ] No credential value is logged or written into the repo.","id":"5ec7-d681-9616-43ed","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","jira","bridge","infra","secrets"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"AWS MCP environment has no Jira credentials, so every bridge tool fails"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787858550964283001,"uuid":"151590e9-0a8d-4bd3-bce1-3d2025c7595a"}